I investigated reachability from the user’s workstation subnet to the internal server VLAN and found no fault. From host_1_1_1_1 (10.1.1.2) I successfully pinged all internal web servers 10.200.0.3–10.200.0.6 with 0% loss (sample RTTs ~0.8–3.4 ms) and the internal DNS server 10.200.0.2 (0% loss, ~1.7 ms). Cross-checks from host_2_1_1_1 (10.2.1.2) to the same servers also showed 0% loss. The topology-wide reachability matrix likewise reports successful ICMP in both directions between hosts and servers. On the user host, the NIC is up and correctly addressed (eth0 10.1.1.2/24), and the default gateway is set to 10.1.1.1; routing is consistent: “default via 10.1.1.1 dev eth0” and local subnet 10.1.1.0/24 present.

Mechanistically, if an uplink or gateway were “wonky,” you would see packet loss or no route; instead, consistent replies from multiple servers across the routed core indicate OSPF/core forwarding is healthy and the host’s gateway path is working. Localization: tests originated from host_1_1_1_1 across the core to the server access switch segment (10.200.0.0/24) and succeeded.

Root cause: No persistent network or configuration fault is present at this time; the reported lockout is not reproducible.

Corrective fix: No changes required. If the issue recurs, capture a timestamped ping/arp from the host, verify eth0 remains up with default via 10.1.1.1, and check for local host firewall/security software temporarily blocking ICMP or transient host-side issues. === END ===